{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1030756833/13/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\" lines=\"26-48\">\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bc93ec48\" lines=\"48-63\">\n<description>\nCopy each foreground object anchored in a true grid corner clockwise into the next corner.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Foreground objects are monochromatic 4-connected components.\n- Relevant objects are exactly those whose bounding box touches two adjacent borders at one grid corner.\n\nRule\n- Start from the input unchanged.\n- For each corner object, copy its pixel mask and color to the next corner clockwise: top-left to top-right, top-right to bottom-right, bottom-right to bottom-left, and bottom-left to top-left.\n- Preserve each object's orientation by aligning its bounding box to the destination corner; stamps may overwrite existing pixels at the destination, but source pixels are not cleared.\n\nKey insight\n- Interior and side-only objects are distractors; only components actually anchored to a corner participate in the clockwise perimeter copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5a719d11\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nWithin each horizontal pair of two-color panels, swap the motif masks and recolor each pasted mask with the source panel's base color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A separator color forms full blank rows and at least one full blank column.\n- The non-separator area is split into row bands, each containing exactly two same-shaped rectangular panels.\n- Each panel has a dominant base color and one minority motif color.\n\nRule:\n- For every row band, clear each panel to its own base color.\n- Paste the right panel's motif mask into the left panel using the right base color.\n- Paste the left panel's motif mask into the right panel using the left base color.\n\nKey insight:\n- The motif colors themselves are distractors; only each motif's shape mask and the two panel base colors are exchanged.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"54dc2872\" lines=\"52-69\">\n<description>\nMove each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls.\n- Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker\n  pixel of a different color.\n\nRule\n- If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,\n  translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel.\n- Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,\n  then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker color pairs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend\n  gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"3cd86f4f\" lines=\"6-18\">\n<description>\nShear the rectangular input down-left onto a black canvas.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid of colored pixels with no internal empty region to infer.\n\nRule\nCreate a black output with the same height and width W + H - 1, then copy\neach input row r unchanged starting at column H - 1 - r.\n\nKey insight\nThe colors and row contents are preserved; only the row offsets change so\nthe left edge forms a descending diagonal.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9841fdad\" lines=\"15-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n  from a blank right panel.\n- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n  connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n- A source run exactly matching the left endpoint slot is copied to the left\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run exactly matching the right endpoint slot is copied to the right\n  two columns of the right active span.\n- A source run connecting the endpoint slots is stretched across the entire\n  right active span.\n\nKey insight:\nThe left panel is a compressed legend for the right panel: endpoint markers\nstay two pixels wide, while connectors between endpoints expand to fill the\ndistance between them.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"880c1354\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nRecolor the separated outer regions by rotating their colors one step clockwise around the yellow-orange divider.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid contains a fixed yellow/orange divider structure.\n- All other colors form separated 4-connected monochrome regions around that divider.\n\nRule:\n- Leave the yellow and orange pixels unchanged.\n- Sort the non-divider regions by their centroid angle around the divider centroid in screen-coordinate clockwise order.\n- Each region keeps its cells and receives the color of the previous region in that cyclic order.\n\nKey insight:\n- The objects do not move; only the color labels cycle through the spatial order of the regions around the divider.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"78e78cff\" lines=\"71-91\">\n<description>\nRecolor the row-wise passage indicated by wall pixels with the singleton marker color.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background contains one singleton marker pixel and one wall color.\n- Wall pixels lie on several rows and form left/right limits around the marker column.\n- Empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows are open horizontal passages.\n\nRule:\n- On a wall row, paint background pixels between the nearest wall strictly\n  left of the marker column and the nearest wall at or right of it; if only\n  one side is present, paint from that wall to the opposite grid edge.\n- On empty rows between the top and bottom wall rows, paint the whole row.\n- On empty rows outside the wall-row band, reuse the nearest end wall-row interval.\n- Preserve wall pixels and the original marker pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The wall color defines scanline limits relative to the marker column; the\n  marker color fills those implied horizontal spans rather than flood-filling\n  through connected background.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4a21e3da\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nCut a sprite along border-control rays and slide pieces to grid corners.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, fg (main object, ~centered), ctrl (1-2 pixels on grid edges).\n\nRule:\n- Draw rays inward from each ctrl pixel along its row/col, painting only bg cells up to the farthest fg pixel (intermittent).\n- Rays partition the grid; fg on the cut line stays in place.\n- Cut pieces in regions touching a control edge slide to that corner; others vanish.\n\nKey insight: Only regions sharing a border edge with a control pixel keep their piece; the ray skips fg cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b9e38dc0\" lines=\"7-18\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
